From 9106f9a1ed6e8696f1a5c174422f761a35df81ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 03:12:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] generic-parallel: raja backend

The behaviours, models and material properties of the generic-parallel
interfaces can be generated for RAJA (`raja` backend), as for Kokkos:

- the points are processed by RAJA::forall with the execution policy given
  by the `execution_policy` option, a type of RAJA copied in the generated
  code. The option is checked before being copied: qualified identifiers,
  integers, `true`, `false` and balanced template arguments only. By
  default, mfront::generic_parallel::raja::DefaultExecutionPolicy
  (MFront/GenericParallel/RAJAExecutionPolicy.hxx):
  RAJA::cuda_exec_async<128> in code compiled for CUDA,
  RAJA::hip_exec_async<128> for HIP, the parallel loop of OpenMP if RAJA is
  built with OpenMP, the sequential loop otherwise. The synchronization is
  explicit, as for the cuda and hip backends;
- the queue of the execution options is a pointer to a resource of camp
  matching the execution policy. If null, the default stream of the current
  device is used with CUDA and HIP, as by the cuda and hip backends (the
  default resource of camp is a stream created by camp on the device 0),
  the default resource of camp otherwise;
- the statistics are updated by the atomic operations of RAJA, only when
  an event occurs at a point. In synchronous mode, they are held by blocks
  of managed memory allocated by the resource, one pool per device, reused
  from one call to another and kept until the end of the process;
- the shared data are copied once per device in the memory of the default
  resource of the execution policy;
- with the execution policies of CUDA and HIP, the stack size of the
  threads is increased if the kernel requires it, as for the cuda, hip and
  kokkos backends (probe kernel never launched);
- the memory space of a library is `device` for the execution policies of
  CUDA and HIP, `host` otherwise. The execution policies of OpenMP target
  and SYCL are refused at compile time: the resources of camp associated
  with them can't allocate the managed memory of the statistics, and the
  `raja` configuration only compiles for CUDA and for the host.

`raja` configuration (cmake): the features of RAJA are read in its
configuration header. If RAJA is built with CUDA, the generated sources are
compiled by nvcc (-x cu, extended lambdas, the host compiler of TFEL,
-forward-unknown-to-host-compiler for the options given by MFront such as
-fPIC) and linked with the shared CUDA runtime, as the calling code;
otherwise by the C++ compiler of TFEL. They are linked with the shared
library of RAJA (a static camp, the default, is included in it; a shared
camp is linked explicitly): a static RAJA is refused, as a RAJA built with
HIP or with clang as the CUDA compiler. The config-raja.json and
config-raja-host.json files use the default execution policy and the
default host execution policy (TFEL_RAJA_CXX_COMPILER, TFEL_RAJA_CXX_FLAGS
and TFEL_RAJA_LINKER_FLAGS substitutions).

Tests: the comparisons of the behaviours with the generic interface (raja,
raja-host, with their TDLS sub-tests) and the tests of the material
properties (raja, raja-host), as for kokkos. The material property tests
also use a resource built on a stream created by the test, and wait for the
asynchronous computations before reading the results. raja-options checks
that valid execution policies are copied in the generated code and that
invalid ones (injected expressions, unbalanced templates, invalid tokens)
are refused, with a script since tfel-check only tolerates the failure of a
command. compare.cxx only checks the preserved stack size for the libraries
computing on the device. With RAJA 2026.07 built with CUDA 13.0 and OpenMP,
all the tests pass on an NVIDIA GPU and on the host.

Documentation: interfaces of the behaviours and of the material
properties, TFEL/Config substitutions, release notes, shared data.
